    "sections": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Venom represents a unique entry in the bayou horror subgenre, following in the footsteps of southern Gothic chillers like <a href=\"/movie/635\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"635\">Angel Heart</a> (1987) and <a href=\"/movie/9913\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"9913\">The Skeleton Key</a> (2005). Set in Louisiana's murky swamplands, the film taps into Vodou mythology and local folklore while delivering slasher-style thrills. Though it didn't break new ground, it came during a wave of supernatural horror films trying to inject fresh elements into familiar formulas, similar to <a href=\"/movie/8922\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"8922\">Jeepers Creepers</a> (2001) and <a href=\"/movie/10066\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"10066\">House of Wax</a> (2005)."
      },
