      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The mid-2000s saw several horror films attempting to merge supernatural elements with slasher conventions. Venom followed this trend but distinguished itself through its Louisiana setting and Vodou elements. While films like <a href=\"/movie/9902\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"9902\">Wrong Turn</a> (2003) and Texas Chainsaw Massacre (2003) relied on backwoods brutality, Venom added mystical elements to its antagonist's origins. The film shares DNA with earlier Southern horrors like <a href=\"/movie/19204\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"19204\">The Beyond</a> (1981) and modern equivalents like Jessabelle (2014)."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Director Jim Gillespie, who previously helmed <a href=\"/movie/3597\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"3597\">I Know What You Did Last Summer</a> (1997), brings similar techniques to Venom's chase sequences and kills. The film's atmosphere owes much to earlier bayou classics like <a href=\"/movie/12528\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"12528\">Southern Comfort</a> (1981) and <a href=\"/movie/635\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"635\">Angel Heart</a> (1987), creating a thick sense of supernatural dread amid the Spanish moss and brackish waters. SUBHEAD: Southern Gothic Horror Evolution"
      },
